<article>
  <div class="tldr">
    <strong>TL;DR</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>OVHcloud's <strong>AI Endpoints</strong> is the only major European cloud with a self-published, per-1M-token LLM price list — and the rates are individually published in <strong>EUR and USD</strong> on a <em>catalog</em> page, not buried in a PDF.</li>
      <li>Four entry points matter to a developer: <strong>Base API</strong> (GA, 400 req/min per project per model), <strong>Batch API</strong> (Beta, 50% off at GA, 24h SLO, 50k rows / 200 MB per file), <strong>free playground</strong> (all models, but 1024 output tokens cap) and an <strong>anonymous tier of 2 req/min per IP</strong> for smoke tests.</li>
      <li>The migration story is a two-line change: the endpoint is OpenAI-compatible, so you swap <code>base_url</code> and the model name. Image generation is the documented exception.</li>
      <li>The real differentiator is <strong>where the data lives</strong>: Gravelines, France, "not stored or shared during or after model use", and the platform is wrapped by Appendix 10 of the OVHcloud Public Cloud Special Conditions.</li>
      <li>The trap is <strong>model lifecycle</strong>: OVHcloud decommissions models on a 3-month public clock and returns <strong>404</strong> after the deadline. Six models are on the current decommission list.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>What OVHcloud AI Endpoints actually is</h2>

  <p>OVHcloud is a French cloud provider that has operated its own datacenters since 1999. AI Endpoints is its serverless inference product: you pick a model from a catalog, create a Public Cloud API key, and call it over an OpenAI-compatible HTTP API. There is no GPU instance to boot, no container to size, and no endpoint to keep warm.</p>

  <p>Two things separate it from the dozen other OpenAI-compatible gateways I have priced for this site. First, it is <strong>operated by the infrastructure owner</strong>, not resold on top of someone else's compute — the inference runs in OVHcloud's own Gravelines datacenter in France. Second, the price list is <strong>published by the vendor in EUR</strong>, per model, per million tokens, on a public page. That sounds unremarkable until you try to price European inference: most EU-sovereign AI offers I have looked at route you to "contact sales".</p>

  <h2>The catalog: what you can actually call</h2>

  <p>The catalog page lists <strong>20 results</strong> as of 2026-09-18, filterable by task (LLM, reasoning, visual, embeddings, speech-to-text, image generation, guardrails, TTS). The current line-up, transcribed from the vendor's own pricing table with the EUR catalog rates beside them:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Model</th><th>Category</th><th>Context</th><th>€ / 1M in</th><th>€ / 1M out</th><th>$ / 1M in</th><th>$ / 1M out</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Qwen3.8-27B</td><td>Visual LLM</td><td>262K</td><td>0.40</td><td>2.70</td><td>$0.47</td><td>$3.19</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Qwen3.6-27B</td><td>Visual LLM</td><td>262K</td><td>0.40</td><td>2.70</td><td>$0.47</td><td>$3.19</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Qwen3.5-397B-A17B</td><td>Visual LLM (MoE)</td><td>262K</td><td>0.60</td><td>3.60</td><td>$0.71</td><td>$4.25</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Qwen3.5-9B</td><td>Visual LLM</td><td>262K</td><td>0.10</td><td>0.15</td><td>$0.12</td><td>$0.18</td></tr>
      <tr><td>gpt-oss-120b</td><td>Reasoning LLM</td><td>131K</td><td>0.08</td><td>0.40</td><td>$0.09</td><td>$0.47</td></tr>
      <tr><td>gpt-oss-20b</td><td>Reasoning LLM</td><td>131K</td><td>0.04</td><td>0.15</td><td>$0.05</td><td>$0.18</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Meta-Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct</td><td>LLM</td><td>131K</td><td>0.67</td><td>0.67</td><td>$0.74</td><td>$0.74</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Qwen2.5-VL-72B-Instruct</td><td>Visual LLM</td><td>32K</td><td>0.91</td><td>0.91</td><td>$1.01</td><td>$1.01</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct</td><td>Code LLM</td><td>—</td><td>0.07</td><td>0.26</td><td>$0.07</td><td>$0.26</td></tr>
